From efffc0c4af3d4372684f9c125df9a125a6d5a5e4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Alia Holes <thealiaholes@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 7 Nov 2026 21:10:35 -0800
Subject: [PATCH 1/6] sms: keep the phone sending live updates, and show when
 it stops

The Android app only forwards new messages after the desktop has sent
request_conversations or request_conversation during the current lifetime
of its SMS plugin. That state is kept in memory on the phone, so after a
reconnect, a re-pair or an app restart the desktop silently stops receiving
messages until someone clicks Refresh in the SMS app.

Ask whenever the link comes up (KdeConnectPlugin::connected), and keep it
alive with a light heartbeat: request_conversation for the newest known
thread with numberToRequest = 1. That sets the same flag on the phone, reads
one message instead of every thread, and always gets exactly one reply, so
the reply doubles as a liveness check. The heartbeat falls back to a full
request_conversations when nothing is cached yet, when the previous request
went unanswered, and every 30 minutes, which also catches a phone that
still answers requests but whose live updates broke.

The daemon records when it last asked, the FIRST request that is still
unanswered (later heartbeats do not move it, only a reply clears it), when
the phone last sent messages and when the link came up, and turns them into
one state (smssyncstatus.h): answered, waiting, never answered on this
connection, or stopped answering. It exposes them over D-Bus (syncStatus(),
syncStatusChanged) and re-evaluates on a timer, because nothing arriving is
exactly the case that has to change the state.

Only a reply clears that clock. It survives reconnects (the plugin is
recreated on every link-up, so it is kept per device for the daemon's
lifetime): a link that drops and comes back faster than the two-minute
window still reaches the warning. Only the time the phone was disconnected
is left out, so a phone that was simply away gets its window back instead
of an instant warning. A link-up inside the request debounce no longer
reads as answered from an older reply; its request is sent when the
debounce ends. A request that cannot even be handed to the link
(sendPacket fails while the device is up) starts the clock too.

When the phone is reachable but has not answered for two minutes, the
daemon raises one desktop notification ("smsSyncStalled", with Retry and
Open Messages), once per stale episode, and closes it when the phone
answers, so a dead sync is visible with the SMS app closed. When the device
disconnects the notification is closed as well (it would otherwise keep
talking about a phone we no longer reach, with dead buttons); when the phone
is back the new link decides afresh. A close that races the notification
server's reply is repeated once.

The SMS app applies the same rule: "Phone answered at HH:MM" (only that; a keep-alive
answer does not prove every thread is current), "Asked the phone ... waiting
for its answer", and the warnings "Not syncing: the phone has not answered
since it connected at HH:MM" / "Not syncing: the phone last answered at
HH:MM" with a Retry action, which stay up until a reply.

Tunable per device in the plugin config: syncHeartbeatSeconds (default
300, 0 disables), syncFullSyncMinutes (default 30, 0 = only on connect) and
syncStaleSeconds (default 120).
